  "slug": "expression-males-0059",
  "summary": "New single-cell and isogenic hiPSC models separate sex chromosome effects from hormones and donor genotype, extending a cross-species prenatal dosage atlas.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is dissecting how sex chromosome complement shapes transcription independent of gonadal hormones. A cross-species single-cell atlas maps sex-biased expression and X-to-autosome dosage compensation across mouse and human prenatal development. In FCG rats, single-cell profiling with mouse comparison indicates that sex chromosome complement dominates gene regulation in the medial septum. An isogenic hiPSC-derived vascular smooth muscle platform contrasts monosomy-X and euploid lines and reports donor-specific transcriptomic effects. Sequencing of aged human nephron segments adds a somatic dimension, with chromosome 18 loss expanding in collecting duct clones. The dioxin-induced fetal uropathy study is largely unrelated to this thread, addressing renal developmental transcriptomics after chemical exposure.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
